PostgreSQL - Altering Tables
1. What is ALTER TABLE?---> ALTER TABLE is used to change the structure of an existing table.
You can use it to:
Add a column
Remove a column
Rename a column
Change a column's data type
Rename a table
Add or remove constraints

i.Add a New Column
Syntax:ALTER TABLE table_name 
ADD COLUMN column_name data_type;
Example:
ALTER TABLE students
ADD COLUMN email VARCHAR(100);
Also add DEFAULT VALUE 
ADD COLUMN email VARCHAR(100) default 'shashi';

Now the table has:
id
name
age
faculty
email


ii. to remove: ALTER TABLE students
DROP COLUMN age;

iii.Rename a Column
Syntax:ALTER TABLE table_name
RENAME COLUMN old_name TO new_name;
Example:ALTER TABLE students
RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;

iv. Add a Column with a Default Value
ALTER TABLE students
ADD COLUMN status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'Active';
Existing rows will get the default value in PostgreSQL.

v. Change Column Data Type: Use ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE.
Example:
ALTER TABLE students
ALTER COLUMN age TYPE BIGINT;
Now the age column uses BIGINT.

Another example
Change name from VARCHAR(100) to TEXT:
ALTER TABLE students
ALTER COLUMN name TYPE TEXT;
PostgreSQL must be able to convert the existing data to the new type.

vi. Add a Constraint
For example, add a UNIQUE constraint:
ALTER TABLE students
ADD CONSTRAINT unique_email UNIQUE (email);
Now duplicate email addresses are not allowed.

vii. Add a NOT NULL Constraint
ALTER TABLE students
ALTER COLUMN name SET NOT NULL;
Now name cannot contain NULL.

viii. Remove a NOT NULL Constraint
ALTER TABLE students
ALTER COLUMN name DROP NOT NULL;
Now name can contain NULL again.

